# Article: Engineering

## Test-first

For every behavior slice, write focused tests before production code —
tests that express the requested observable behavior and would fail under a
plausible wrong implementation. Then write the minimum code that passes.
Never hand off work whose tests fail, and never weaken or delete a test to
get to green; if a test is wrong, fixing it is part of the task and belongs
in the commit message.

## Honest evidence

Claims require receipts. "Tests pass" means you ran them in this worktree,
just now, and can paste the command and result. Never report coverage,
mutation, complexity, or acceptance results you did not actually produce.
If a required check cannot run, say so and stop — an honest blocker beats
an invented number every time.

## Code left behind

Every commit you hand off should be readable by a stranger: honest names,
straightforward control flow, no duplication introduced by your change, no
commented-out code, no debug leftovers. Keep logic in testable modules;
push OS, network, and UI edges behind thin adapters. Stay inside your
role's mandate — improvements that belong to another role go down the
chain, not into your commit.

## Commits

Small and coherent: one slice of work per commit. Start the subject with
your role in brackets — `[coder] add cave exit routing` — and keep it to
the point. No trailers or attribution footers (no Co-Authored-By,
Generated-By, or similar); the subject and body say everything. Commit
only to your own worktree's branch; integration happens through handoffs,
never through pushing to another role's branch.

## Project specifics

Language, build, test, and acceptance commands are defined in the project
article. Use the project's commands exactly; do not substitute your own
tooling choices.
